 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Establishes the metropolitan transportation authority riders' council for persons with disabilities; provides for membership and powers and duties.",
 "title": "Establishes metropolitan transportation authority disabled riders' council",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A5267",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A5267-2015",
     "printNo": "A5267",
     "session": 2015,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                          VETO MESSAGE - No.229\n\nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nAssembly Bill Number 5267, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  public authorities law, in relation to the\n      board  of  the  metropolitan  transportation  authority;  and   in\n      relation to the establishment of the metropolitan transit authori-\n      ty riders' council for people with disabilities\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This  bill  would amend the Public Authorities Law to add a third non-\nvoting member to the Metropolitan Transportation Authority (MTA)  board,\nand  to  create  a new MTA Riders' Council for Persons with Disabilities\nconsisting of fifteen members who are  persons  with  disabilities  that\nregularly use the MTA transit system.\n\n  While  the  goal  of  this bill is laudable, persons with disabilities\nthat utilize the MTA are  already  well-represented  on  three  existing\nstatutory  councils:  the New York City Transit Authority Advisory Coun-\ncil, the Metro-North Rail Commuter Council, and  the  Long  Island  Rail\nRoad  Commuter  Council.  Together,  they make up the Permanent Citizens\nAdvisory Committee (PCAC), an independent entity that has  a  non-voting\nseat on the MTA Board.\n\n  Moreover, the MTA is already subject to federal and state law require-\nments,  most  notably  Title  II  of the Americans with Disabilities Act\n(ADA) and Federal Transit Administration regulations. Further,  the  New\nYork  City  Transit, an MTA subsidiary, continuously receives input from\nthe Paratransit Advisory Committee, which is  comprised  exclusively  of\nmembers  who  either  use  the  MTA's  paratransit services or represent\norganizations for persons with disabilities. For  these  reasons,  I  am\ntherefore constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 229,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2015
   }
